    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d216">

                <pb facs="0445" n="159" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg159"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/2384</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-02-05T15:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 5, 1916, 3 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received February 6, 9 a.
                        m.</hi>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3445. Ambassador Morgenthau yesterday was
                    introduced by me to Von Jagow and had
                    long talk with him. He also had an interview with Zimmermann not arranged by me. His report may interest you.
                        Morgenthau tells me that Zimmermann asked him if it was not a fact that
                    the German-Americans in America would rise to rebellion in case of trouble
                    between Germany and America. Von Jagow
                    said that Germany had never agreed to give up their torpedoing vessels without
                    notice, but had merely stated that orders had been given not to torpedo certain
                    vessels without notice, and that Germany reserved the right to change these
                    orders at any time.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
